WEBVTT

00:00:00.744 --> 00:00:02.619
Semicolons and colons.

00:00:02.619 --> 00:00:04.769
A semicolon works like a period.

00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:06.886
A colon says: here's what I mean.

00:00:08.444 --> 00:00:10.169
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:13.219
The choices offer semicolons and colons.

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:16.097
And the blank sits between two parts of the sentence.

00:00:17.126 --> 00:00:20.531
It's testing the exact job of each punctuation mark.

00:00:21.577 --> 00:00:22.852
Here's the method.

00:00:22.852 --> 00:00:25.627
For a semicolon, test both sides.

00:00:25.627 --> 00:00:27.902
Each one must be a full sentence.

00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:30.237
For a colon, test the left side.

00:00:30.237 --> 00:00:32.162
It must be a full sentence.

00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:33.937
Then check the right side.

00:00:33.937 --> 00:00:36.587
Does it explain, list, or give an example?

00:00:36.587 --> 00:00:38.247
That's a colon's job.

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:40.755
And never put a colon straight after a verb.

00:00:41.839 --> 00:00:45.614
Rule one: a semicolon works exactly like a period.

00:00:45.614 --> 00:00:49.914
Because everyone left can't stand alone, so the semicolon fails.

00:00:49.914 --> 00:00:51.555
Everyone had left can.

00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:56.314
Rule two: a colon needs a full sentence before it.

00:00:56.314 --> 00:01:00.004
The ingredients are, isn't complete, so the colon fails.

00:01:00.004 --> 00:01:01.086
Just drop it.

00:01:02.173 --> 00:01:07.128
Rule three: after a colon comes an explanation, a list, or an example.

00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:09.956
The cause was clear: the battery overheated.

00:01:11.014 --> 00:01:12.264
Let's try one.

00:01:12.264 --> 00:01:15.954
The left side: the ship's log revealed the cause of the delay.

00:01:15.954 --> 00:01:17.629
That's a full sentence.

00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:20.159
The right side tells us what the cause was.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:21.384
It explains.

00:01:21.384 --> 00:01:23.659
A comma alone is a splice.

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:25.909
No punctuation is a run-on.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:29.284
Adding and turns the explanation into a separate fact.

00:01:29.284 --> 00:01:31.984
A colon introduces the explanation.

00:01:31.984 --> 00:01:33.365
The answer is B.

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:38.009
Watch out for a colon after a verb, or after such as.

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:40.784
The left side isn't a complete sentence.

00:01:40.784 --> 00:01:43.369
The kit includes, colon, is wrong.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:44.698
No colon needed.

00:01:45.744 --> 00:01:47.614
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:01:47.614 --> 00:01:48.674
How to spot it.

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:50.024
The four steps.

00:01:50.024 --> 00:01:51.129
The rules.

00:01:51.129 --> 00:01:52.154
And the trap.

